%%textfont Helvetica 10
%%begintext
R&O XXVIII, version 4

This tune derives from a number of similar tunes played in southern Illinois in the early 1900's.
Lotus Dickey (1911-1989) learned this breakdown from Allen Downey, but forgot it's name, and as a
result, his version was called "Dickey's Discovery".  Lotus Dickey then taught his version to Pete 
Sutherland, who taught it to Garrett Moore, who taught it to Pat Palmer, who transcribed this version.

The A part appears in Stacy Phillips' book "Traditional American Fiddle Tunes vol. 1" as Brisk Young Soldier,
but the B parts are different.  A version of this tune appears as King's Lament in Gene Silverberg's book 
"Tunes I Learned at Tractor Tavern".
%%endtext
% Source: sources/harbormist-com/tunes1/abc.zip/abc/Dickeys_Discovery.txt.abc

