| "G"ddd d/d/ | BGG G/G/ | "G"Bd/d/ ed| "D"cBA A |
w: Fox went out on a | chil-ly night, and he | prayed to the moon to | give him light, said |
%%vskip 10
| "G"BB/B/"G7"BB | "C"cc"Am"cd | "D"BB    AA | "G"G2G2 | "D"   A2A2   | "G"(Bd)"G7"d2 ||
w: I've ma-ny miles to | go this night be- | fore I reach the | town O | town O | town ~ O |
%%vskip 10
| "C"ee/e/"Am"ge | "G"dd"Em"BG | "Am"Ac"D"BA | "G"G2G2- | "G/F#"G4- | "C/E"G4-   | "D7"G4- |]
w: I've ma-ny miles to | go this night be- | fore I reach the | town O |      |     |     |
%%vskip 10

%%textfont Helvetica 12
%%begintext


  He ran til he came to the farmer's bin,
  Where the geese and the ducks were kept penned in. Said,
  A couple of you are gonna grease my chin before I leave this town-O, town-O, town-O.
  A couple of you are gonna grease my chin before I leave this town-O.

  He grabbed a gray goose by neck, 
  And he slung that duck across his back. Well,
  He didn't mind the quack-quack-quack or the legs all dangling down-o, down-o, down. No,
  He didn't mind the quack-quack-quack or the legs all dangling down-o, down-o, down-o.


  The farmer's wife jumped out of bed.
  She ran to the window and popped out her head.  She cried,
  John, John the gray goose is gone, and the fox is on the town-o, town-o, town-o
  John, John the great goose is gone, and the fox is on the town-o.


  John, he ran to the top of the hill,
  And he blew his horn both loud and shrill.
  The fox, he said, I better go with my kill, they'll soon be on my trail-O, trail-O, trail-O.
  The fox, he said, I better go with my kill, they'll soon be on my trail-O.


  He ran til he came to his nice warm den,
  And there sat his little ones, eight, nine, ten. They said
  Daddy, Daddy, better go back again, 'cause it must have been a mighty fine town-O, town-O, town-O.
  Daddy, Daddy, better go back again, 'cause it must have been a mighty fine town-O.


  The fox and his wife, without any strife,
  Tore up that goose without a fork or a knife. Well,
  They'd never had such a dinner in their lives, and the little ones chewed on the bones-O, bones-O, bones-O.
  They'd never had such a dinner in their lives, and the little ones chewed on the bones-O.

%%endtext

