%%textfont Helvetica 12
%%begintext

A popular new old-time fiddle tune, composed in the 1980's by old-time fiddler and luthier Joe Thrift of Dobson, 
Surry County, NC, and recorded by Thrift's main band, the Red Hots, on their CD "Ready to Roll" in 1989 and 
released on Fire Ant Records in 1995.  
%%endtext

%%textfont Helvetica 12
%%begintext
The title was inspired by the hat which Keith Brand, a radio personality from Philadelphia, regularly wore to 
old-time festivals such as Galax during the mid-1980s.  The "Whiteface" on the hat (which Brand still had as of 
2010) most likely refers to the Hereford breed of beef cattle. Native to England, they have red-colored bodies 
and white faces, and are often referred to as "white-faced cattle."
%%endtext

%%textfont Helvetica 12
%%begintext
This version has been squared off, re-chorded for contra-dances, and coded as abc by Bill Quern.   Bill plays 
this version on banjo in Last Chance tuning; chords and background information via Sarah Gowan.  Hear it on 
Youtube at http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=nIvEseJNRLw .
%%endtext
% Source: sources/harbormist-com/tunes1/abc.zip/abc/Whiteface.txt.abc

